Ally orchestrates third-party accessibility engines and libraries. They are
consumed as dependencies, are not redistributed as part of this source tree,
and remain under their own licenses:

  Accessibility engines

  - axe-core (Deque Systems)              MPL-2.0
    https://github.com/dequelabs/axe-core
    Consumed as `axe-core`; its bundled `axe.source` is injected into the page
    under audit.

  - IBM Equal Access (IBM)                Apache-2.0
    https://github.com/IBMa/equal-access
    Consumed as `accessibility-checker-engine`, the dependency-free browser
    bundle, which is injected into the page under audit. The `accessibility-
    checker` harness is deliberately not used, because it bundles its own
    Puppeteer and Chromedriver.

  - Siteimprove Alfa (Siteimprove)        MIT
    https://github.com/Siteimprove/alfa
    Consumed as `@siteimprove/alfa-act`, `@siteimprove/alfa-dom`,
    `@siteimprove/alfa-rules`, `@siteimprove/alfa-web` and
    `@siteimprove/alfa-playwright`. Runs in Node against a serialized copy of
    the page.

  - QualWeb                               ISC
    https://github.com/qualweb/qualweb
    Consumed as `@qualweb/act-rules`, `@qualweb/wcag-techniques`,
    `@qualweb/best-practices`, `@qualweb/qw-page`, `@qualweb/util` and
    `@qualweb/locale` — the browser bundles QualWeb's own core injects. The
    `@qualweb/core` package is deliberately not used, because it drives its own
    Puppeteer instance.

  Supporting libraries

  - tabbable (David Clark and contributors)  MIT
    https://github.com/focus-trap/tabbable
    Used by `@ally/analyzer-keyboard` to determine the expected sequential
    focus order.

  - Playwright (Microsoft)                Apache-2.0
    https://github.com/microsoft/playwright

  - Astro                                 MIT
    https://github.com/withastro/astro

  - Tailwind CSS                          MIT
    https://github.com/tailwindlabs/tailwindcss

No engine source is vendored or relicensed. Ally is not affiliated with or
endorsed by any of these projects, and none of them endorse Ally's normalized
findings, its deduplication decisions or its Automated Accessibility Score.
